  "hints": [
    "The brute force checks every later day. Which earlier days are still waiting for an answer when you reach day j?",
    "Keep a stack of indices that have not yet seen a higher price. Their prices are nonincreasing from bottom to top.",
    "At day j, pop every index whose price is lower than prices[j] and set its answer to j - index. Then push j."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function daysUntilHigher(prices) {\n  const answer = new Array(prices.length).fill(0)\n  const waiting = []\n  for (let j = 0; j < prices.length; j++) {\n    while (waiting.length && prices[waiting[waiting.length - 1]] < prices[j]) {\n      const i = waiting.pop()\n      answer[i] = j - i\n    }\n    waiting.push(j)\n  }\n  return answer\n}\n",
    "py": "def days_until_higher(prices):\n    answer = [0] * len(prices)\n    waiting = []\n    for j, price in enumerate(prices):\n        while waiting and prices[waiting[-1]] < price:\n            i = waiting.pop()\n            answer[i] = j - i\n        waiting.append(j)\n    return answer\n",
    "explanation": "A monotonic stack holds days still waiting for a higher price, in nonincreasing price order. A new price resolves every waiting day with a lower price (they pop off the top), then waits itself. Days left on the stack at the end keep answer 0."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n)",
    "notes": "Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so the nested loop is linear overall."
  },
